<TopicPage slug="subjects/statistics/expert/multivariate-statistics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Multivariate statistics analyses <strong>many variables simultaneously</strong> — because real
    things have many attributes at once, and one-variable-at-a-time analysis both misses
    interactions and drowns in repeated tests. It is also the mathematical home of modern machine
    learning: regression with many predictors, classification, and dimension reduction are all
    multivariate statistics under different marketing names.
  </p>
  <p>
    The route there runs through the two-variable case, which is why this lesson's syllabus
    foundation is the bivariate toolkit: naming a dependent variable (the outcome, on the
    y-axis) and independent variables (the predictors, on the x-axis), reading a scatter
    diagram's direction and strength, fitting a line of best fit, and knowing exactly when
    a fitted line may be trusted. Multivariate analysis is not a different subject — it is
    that same toolkit, done honestly when the number of variables exceeds the number of
    intuitions.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four ideas that organise high-dimensional data" widget="equationStep" config={multivariateCfg} />

  <h2>Scatter diagrams and correlation: the foundation, read properly</h2>
  <p>
    Every multivariate method begins as a scatter diagram that got too big to draw. In the
    two-variable case the discipline is visual first: plot the pairs, read the
    <em>direction</em> (positive — both rise together; negative — one falls as the other
    rises; or none), the <em>form</em> (linear or curved — a straight-line summary of a
    curved cloud is a lie), and the <em>strength</em> (tight cloud versus diffuse). The
    Pearson correlation coefficient compresses all of that into one number between −1 and
    1: r = 0.9 is a strong positive linear association, r = −0.2 weak and negative, r
    close to 0 no <em>linear</em> association. Its most useful transformation is r²: a
    correlation of 0.8 means r² = 0.64 — about 64% of the outcome's variation travels with
    the predictor, and 36% does not. The multivariate generalisation is the
    <strong>correlation matrix</strong>: an n-variable dataset becomes an n-by-n table of
    every pairwise r, the first honest look at any high-dimensional dataset — and the
    place where multicollinearity (predictors that are nearly copies of each other) is
    spotted before it poisons a regression.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that a strong correlation licenses a causal claim.
  Ice-cream sales correlate with drowning deaths — because both are driven by summer
  heat; the confounder, not the ice cream, moves both. Correlation is evidence of
  association; causation requires a mechanism, an experiment or a designed identification
  strategy (the causal-inference lesson's territory).</p>

  <h2>Fitting the line: by eye, by semi-averages, by least squares</h2>
  <p>
    The syllabus's fitting sequence is a staircase toward the real method. <strong>By
    eye</strong>: lay a ruler through the cloud balancing the points above and below —
    quick, subjective, fine for a first look. <strong>By semi-averages</strong>: split the
    data into two halves (by ordered x), average x and y within each half, and draw the
    line through the two mean-points — crude but reproducible. Worked example: eight
    pupils' study hours versus test scores; the lower half averages (2.5 hours, 55
    marks), the upper half (7.5 hours, 77 marks). Slope = (77 − 55) ÷ (7.5 − 2.5) =
    4.4 marks per hour; the line through the lower mean-point gives intercept 55 − 4.4 ×
    2.5 = 44. So the model reads: <em>each extra study hour is worth about 4.4 marks,
    from a baseline of 44</em>. <strong>By least squares</strong> — the method all
    software runs — chooses the line minimising the sum of squared vertical errors,
    which is optimal when the scatter is Gaussian; with many predictors (multiple
    regression) the same idea extends to a plane, then a hyperplane, and the fitted
    equation y = a + b₁x₁ + b₂x₂ + … + bₖxₖ keeps the same grammar: each coefficient is
    that variable's estimated effect <em>holding the others fixed</em>.
  </p>

  <h2>Assumptions and the limits of extrapolation</h2>
  <p>
    A fitted line is a claim with conditions: the relationship is genuinely
    <em>linear</em> in the range observed; the errors are independent and roughly
    constant-spread (no funnel shapes in the residuals); and no single point is quietly
    running the show. The classic demonstration is Anscombe's quartet (1973): four tiny
    datasets with <em>identical</em> means, identical correlation (r ≈ 0.816) and the
    identical fitted line y = 3 + 0.5x — yet one is a clean linear cloud, one sits on a
    perfect curve, one has a single outlier dragging the line, and one has a vertical
    spike. Same statistics, four different realities: the lesson is that the summary
    numbers certify nothing until the scatter diagram is looked at — which is why
    residual plots, not just summaries, are part of any honest fit.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Extrapolation</strong> is the second limit: a line fitted on data between
    x = 2 and x = 8 hours says nothing defensible about x = 20 — the relationship may
    curve, saturate or reverse outside the observed range (the semi-average model above
    would confidently predict 132 marks at 20 hours, past every exam's maximum). The
    habit to install: state the data's range, and treat any prediction beyond it as a
    hypothesis, not a forecast. In multivariate work the failure mode compounds —
    prediction surfaces extrapolate in directions you cannot even plot.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that a high r² means the model is correct. Anscombe's
  quartet makes the point exactly: r² = 0.67 in all four datasets, one of which is a
  perfect parabola that the straight line misdescribes. Fit statistics measure how well
  <em>this functional form</em> summarises the data — not whether that form is true.</p>

  <h2>Many variables at once: the methods that scale</h2>
  <p>
    With many predictors the bivariate toolkit generalises along three axes.
    <strong>Multiple regression and the GLM family</strong> handle many predictors, one
    outcome — including logistic regression when the outcome is yes/no — and remain
    applied statistics' backbone. <strong>Dimension reduction</strong> handles the case
    where predictors outnumber insights: principal component analysis (PCA) rotates the
    data onto directions of greatest variance, and routinely 50 correlated variables
    collapse to 3–5 components carrying most of the story (face recognition's
    "eigenfaces" and market research's brand dimensions are both PCA in costume).
    <strong>Classification and clustering</strong> handle group structure: with known
    labels, discriminant analysis and logistic regression draw the boundaries
    (supervised); without labels, k-means and hierarchical clustering find them
    (unsupervised) — the distinction machine learning renamed but did not invent.
    <strong>MANOVA and mixed models</strong> extend group comparison to several outcomes
    at once and to hierarchical data — pupils in classes in schools, athletes in squads —
    the standard machinery of modern clinical and education research.
  </p>

  <h2>A worked example: thirteen measurements, two components</h2>
  <p>
    PCA's behaviour is easiest to trust on a concrete case. Measure a squad of 200
    athletes on 13 anthropometric variables — height, weight, sitting height, arm span,
    six skinfold sites, three limb circumferences. The correlation matrix shows the
    expected structure: the length variables correlate strongly with each other, the
    fat variables with each other, and the two clusters weakly with each other. PCA
    formalises the hunch: the first component comes out as an approximately equal
    weighting of the length and mass variables — a general <em>size</em> axis — and
    accounts for perhaps 60% of the total variance; the second contrasts the fat
    variables against the lean ones — a <em>composition</em> axis — adding another
    20%. Two axes, constructed from the data rather than assumed, now carry about
    80% of thirteen variables' story, and each athlete is summarised by two scores:
    how big, and what made of. Every downstream analysis (comparing age groups,
    plotting squads, screening outliers) runs on those two scores instead of
    thirteen, with the honest caveat that the components are mathematical
    conveniences — they are patterns of variance, not guaranteed to be the variables
    that matter for performance.
  </p>

  <h2>Classification: drawing boundaries in high dimensions</h2>
  <p>
    Where PCA summarises, classification decides. The supervised case: given known
    groups — respond to a treatment or not, churn or stay, sprinter or endurance
    profile — fit a rule that assigns new cases. Logistic regression makes the rule
    interpretable: it models the log-odds of membership as a weighted sum of
    predictors, and each coefficient reads directly — a predictor with coefficient
    0.7 multiplies the odds by roughly 2 (the exponential of 0.7) per unit increase,
    other variables held fixed. Linear discriminant analysis does the same job with
    distributional assumptions, and modern descendants (random forests, gradient
    boosting — the machine-learning ladder's territory) trade some interpretability
    for boundary flexibility. The unsupervised case: k-means partitions the data into
    k clusters by iteratively assigning points to the nearest centre and re-centring;
    the honest difficulties are choosing k (elbow and silhouette diagnostics, not
    revelation) and remembering that clusters are patterns in <em>your</em> variables —
    change the variables, change the clusters.
  </p>

  <h2>Validation: why in-sample success lies</h2>
  <p>
    The multivariate habit that separates the professional from the enthusiastic
    amateur is <em>validation on data the model never saw</em>. Fit a regression with
    50 predictors on 200 observations and the in-sample fit can look splendid —
    with enough variables, a linear model memorises noise the way a student
    memorises past papers. The honest protocol: hold out a test set (or rotate
    folds — k-fold cross-validation), fit on the remainder, and score the held-out
    data; the gap between in-sample and out-of-sample performance is the
    overfitting tax, and it grows with every variable added and every observation
    removed. This is also why the curse of dimensionality (below) is a budgeting
    problem: dimension reduction and regularisation are how the tax is paid down,
    and out-of-sample validation is how you know whether it has been.
  </p>

  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that adding variables always improves the model.
  In-sample it does — mechanically, R² can only rise — but each addition costs a
  degree of freedom, invites multicollinearity (coefficients that swing wildly because
  two predictors carry the same information), and buys memorised noise that will not
  repeat out of sample. The professional reflex runs the other way: fit the smallest
  model the evidence supports, and make any addition justify itself on held-out data.</p>
  <p>
    The practical toolkit for the multicollinearity problem: standardise predictors so
    coefficients are comparable, inspect variance inflation factors (a common rule of
    thumb worries above 5–10), and when predictors are near-duplicates, prefer
    combining or dropping them to pretending the model can split their credit. The
    multivariate discipline, in one line: every variable earns its place, every
    coefficient gets an interval, and every claim is checked on data the fit never
    touched.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Two frontiers separate competence from expertise. <strong>The curse of
    dimensionality</strong>: as variables multiply, volume explodes and data becomes
    sparse — nearest neighbours stop being near, distances stop discriminating, and the
    sample needed to fill a p-dimensional grid grows exponentially in p. The
    consequences are practical: high-dimensional models overfit (they memorise noise
    that, in high dimensions, looks like signal), which is why regularisation
    (ridge, lasso — shrinking coefficients) and dimension reduction are not optional
    refinements but the price of admission, and why "more variables" is not the same
    as "more information". <strong>The multiple-testing burden</strong>: test 10,000
    genes for association at the 5% level and about 500 "significant" hits appear by
    pure chance — the false discovery rate procedure (Benjamini–Hochberg, 1995) controls
    the expected share of false positives among declared discoveries, and is the quiet
    reason large-scale science is possible at all. Both frontiers say the same thing
    from different directions: high dimensions are where intuition fails first and
    discipline matters most — the multivariate toolkit is that discipline, formalised.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Johnson & Wichern, Applied Multivariate Statistical Analysis; Hastie, Tibshirani & Friedman, ESL.</p>
</TopicPage>
